  "documentation": {
    "range": null,
    "population": "In North America, the total population size is estimated at 410,000 mature individuals in the USA and Canada (Partners in Flight 2024). This region forms the entirety of the species' global range. The global population size is estimated at 450,000 mature individuals (Partners in Flight 2024) and has been placed in the band of 100,000-499,999 mature individuals.",
    "habitats": null,
    "threats": null,
    "measures": null,
    "use_trade": "This species has been reported as used in trade at a high prevalence, being found in three of the seven trade datasets evaluated (Donald&#160;<em>et al. </em>2024).",
    "rationale": "This species has an extremely large range, and hence does not approach the thresholds for Vulnerable under the range size criterion (extent of occurrence under 20,000 km² combined with a declining or fluctuating range size, habitat extent/quality, or population size and a small number of locations or severe fragmentation). The population size is very large, and hence does not approach the thresholds for Vulnerable under the population size criterion (under 10,000 mature individuals with a continuing decline estimated to be over 10% in ten years or three generations, or with a specified population structure). The population trend appears to be increasing, and hence the species does not approach the thresholds for Vulnerable under the population trend criterion (over 30% decline over ten years or three generations). For these reasons the species is evaluated as Least Concern.",
    "trend_justification": "The species' population in North America (USA and Canada) is estimated to have a positive annual trend (Partners in Flight 2024), which equates to a rapid increase in population size over three generations (43.5 years). However, the Waterbird Populations Portal considers the overall population trend uncertain (Wetlands International 2023). As North America holds the vast majority of the species' global range, the global population size is here considered to be increasing.",
    "taxonomic_notes": null
  },
